k-expert-skip-bench_help
usage: k-expert-skip-bench [-h] --model MODEL --prompts PROMPTS --output-dir
                           OUTPUT_DIR [--arms-file ARMS_FILE]
                           [--arms ARMS [ARMS ...]]
                           [--batches BATCHES [BATCHES ...]]
                           [--repeats REPEATS] [--newtokens NEWTOKENS]
                           [--prompt-tokens PROMPT_TOKENS]
                           [--prompt-offset PROMPT_OFFSET]
                           [--warmup-seconds WARMUP_SECONDS]
                           [--warmup-tokens WARMUP_TOKENS] [--dtype DTYPE]
                           [--quantization QUANTIZATION]
                           [--max-model-len MAX_MODEL_LEN]
                           [--gpu-memory-utilization GPU_MEMORY_UTILIZATION]
                           [--backend {torch,triton}] [--seed SEED]
                           [--nvml-device NVML_DEVICE] [--no-energy]
                           [--traceonly] [--trace-steps TRACE_STEPS]
                           [--capture-traces] [--dense-counter-check]
                           [--execution {eager,graph}] [--graph-counter-pass]

Single loaded vLLM 0.26 eager engine: native decode TPOT, energy and routes.
Requires an external GPU lock (run_locked.py). Does not obtain or bypass a
lock. Performance and energy/trace are separate passes. Forced-length greedy
decoding is a workload-controlled benchmark, not natural termination or task
quality. NVML measures whole-board decode energy, not process-only power or
HBM bytes. v0.26 API checked against vllm/v1/engine/llm_engine.py at tag
v0.26.0: add_request returns an internal ID; outputs retain the caller's
external ID. V1 multiprocessing and asynchronous scheduling are disabled
before loading.

